---
import MultiLineChart from "../components/MultiLineChart.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import {
  CENTRAL_ERROR_SCALE,
  DEGREES_OF_FREEDOM,
  ELIGIBILITY_DATE,
  HALF_LIFE_DAYS,
  MINIMUM_RUNOFF_POLLS,
  RUNOFF_CORRELATION_WITH_LE_PEN,
  RUNOFF_ERROR_STANDARD_DEVIATION,
  SIMULATIONS,
} from "../../scripts/election-2027/simulate";
import { formatDate } from "../utils/date";
import { forecast, formatProbability, getFavourite, getHistorySeries, getSensitivityRange } from "../utils/election";

/** The first round has no official date yet; April is all that is known. */
const FIRST_ROUND_MONTH = "2027-04-01";
const asOf = new Date(forecast.asOf);
const monthsToGo = Math.round((Date.parse(FIRST_ROUND_MONTH) - asOf.getTime()) / (30.44 * 86_400_000));

const favourite = getFavourite();
const challenger = forecast.candidates[1];
const likeliestDuel = forecast.duels[0];
const likeliestOpponent = likeliestDuel?.candidates.find((candidate) => candidate !== favourite.candidate);
const range = getSensitivityRange();
const series = getHistorySeries();
const firstPoint = forecast.history[0];
const opponentSentence =
  likeliestDuel && likeliestOpponent
    ? `Her most likely opponent is ${likeliestOpponent} (${formatProbability(likeliestDuel.probability)} of runs).`
    : "";
const challengerSentence = challenger
  ? `The strongest challenger is ${challenger.candidate}, who reaches the runoff in ${formatProbability(challenger.qualifyProbability)} of runs and wins in ${formatProbability(challenger.winProbability)}.`
  : "";
const simulationCount = SIMULATIONS.toLocaleString("en-US");
const largestOverstatement = Math.max(...forecast.sensitivity.map((cell) => cell.lePenPollOverstatement));
/** 95th percentile of a unit-variance Student-t with 5 degrees of freedom: half of a 90% range. */
const STUDENT_T_90_HALF_WIDTH = 1.56;
const exampleShare = favourite.firstRoundMean / 100;
const exampleRange = Math.round(
  STUDENT_T_90_HALF_WIDTH * CENTRAL_ERROR_SCALE * Math.sqrt(exampleShare * (1 - exampleShare)) * 100,
);
---

<Layout
  title="2027 election forecast - Theo Dammaretz"
  description={`A poll-based simulation of the 2027 French presidential election: ${favourite.candidate} wins in ${formatProbability(favourite.winProbability)} of runs.`}
  backLink="/"
>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <h1>2027 election forecast</h1>
    <p class="meta">Updated {formatDate(asOf)}</p>

    <h2>Why this page exists</h2>
    <p>
      Every new poll for 2027 comes with a headline, and I kept wondering what all of them, taken together, actually
      say about who wins. So I wrote a small model that takes the polls listed on{" "}
      <a href="https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liste_de_sondages_sur_l%27%C3%A9lection_pr%C3%A9sidentielle_fran%C3%A7aise_de_2027"
        >French Wikipedia</a
      >, simulates the election {simulationCount} times and counts who comes out on top. This page is where the result
      lives, and it moves whenever a new poll lands.
    </p>
    <p>
      Please take it with a generous grain of salt. I am not a pollster, the model is a few hundred lines of
      TypeScript, and the first round is still about {monthsToGo} months away. Polls this early have a poor track
      record (ask François Fillon in 2017, or Éric Zemmour and Valérie Pécresse in 2022). What this page measures is
      what the polls imply <em>today</em>, not what will happen in April.
    </p>

    <h2>Where things stand</h2>
    <p>
      {favourite.candidate} wins in <strong>{formatProbability(favourite.winProbability)}</strong> of the simulated
      elections. Her first-round score is centred on {favourite.firstRoundMean}% and lands between {
        favourite.firstRoundLow
      }% and {favourite.firstRoundHigh}% in nine runs out of ten, and she reaches the runoff in {
        formatProbability(favourite.qualifyProbability)
      } of them.{" "}
      {opponentSentence}{" "}
      {challengerSentence}
    </p>
    <p>
      That {formatProbability(favourite.winProbability)} rests on my central assumptions. With a wider polling error in
      the runoff, and polls overstating {favourite.candidate} by up to {largestOverstatement} points (as they did in 2017 and 2022), it
      falls to {formatProbability(range.low)}. So the honest confidence band is <strong
        >{formatProbability(range.low)} to {formatProbability(range.high)}</strong
      >: a clear favourite, but nowhere near a done deal.
    </p>

    <MultiLineChart
      title="Chance of winning, by date"
      series={series}
      startDate={ELIGIBILITY_DATE}
      annotation={{ date: ELIGIBILITY_DATE, label: "Le Pen confirmed" }}
      caption={`Each point reruns the model with only the polls published by that day. The line starts on ${
        firstPoint ? formatDate(new Date(firstPoint.date)) : "the first usable day"
      }, once enough runoff polls existed to model her likely duels. "Others" adds up every remaining candidate.`}
    />

    <h2>Methodology and math</h2>
    <p>
      The model is a Monte Carlo simulation: rather than solving the election on paper, it plays it out {
        simulationCount
      } times with a different random draw each time, and the share of runs a candidate wins is their probability. Only
      polls from {formatDate(new Date(ELIGIBILITY_DATE))} onwards count, the day {favourite.candidate}'s candidacy was
      confirmed on appeal; that is {forecast.waveCount} poll waves and {forecast.scenarioCount} first-round scenarios so
      far. Each run goes through four steps.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Who runs.</strong> Pollsters test several lists of candidates in each survey, because nobody knows yet who
        will stand. Each run picks one of those scenarios at random. A survey's weight halves every {HALF_LIFE_DAYS} days,
        and is split evenly between its scenarios, so a firm testing six lists does not count six times.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>First-round error.</strong> Each candidate's share <var>p</var> gets a random error with a standard deviation
        of <var>k</var>·√(<var>p</var>(1 − <var>p</var>)), with <var>k</var> = {CENTRAL_ERROR_SCALE}. For a candidate
        polling {favourite.firstRoundMean}% that is roughly ±{exampleRange} points nine times out of ten. The error follows a Student-t distribution with {
          DEGREES_OF_FREEDOM
        } degrees of freedom rather than a bell curve, so big misses are rarer than small ones but far from impossible.
        Shares are then rescaled to add up to 100%.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Runoff.</strong> The top two meet in the second round. Their starting point is the recency-weighted average
        of their head-to-head polls, plus an error of {RUNOFF_ERROR_STANDARD_DEVIATION} points (standard deviation). That
        error is {RUNOFF_CORRELATION_WITH_LE_PEN * 100}% correlated with how far {favourite.candidate} beat or missed her first-round
        polls in the same run: if the polls underestimate her once, they probably do it twice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Untested duels.</strong> A pairing with fewer than {MINIMUM_RUNOFF_POLLS} runoff polls has no reliable starting
        point, so runs that reach it are dropped and the probabilities are computed on the rest ({
          formatProbability(forecast.droppedShare)
        } of runs today).
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>A few things the model does not do, and that you should keep in mind:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        French polls use quota samples, so their published margins of error have no strict statistical basis. The error
        sizes above are my assumptions, not values computed from sample sizes.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dropping untested duels slightly favours {favourite.candidate}: the runs that disappear are mostly ones where she
        misses the runoff.
      </li>
      <li>There is no correction for pollster house effects, and the model assumes she will be on the ballot.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .meta {
    opacity: 0.6;
    margin-top: -0.5rem;
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
